#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096502 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "96967f25-565a-5338-878e-00fd30055772" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area Bounded Below a Curve and Above the X-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "dc29cf6e-4f68-552e-ae7a-f9834eec1bd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-781" :question/problem "Find the area between the curve $y=x^2-2x+2$, the $x$-axis, and the lines $x=0$ and $x=2$.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1040/Source/Images/cd089b9532fe77a9dea0d813bf425efc.png)" :question/worked-solution "To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∫_{a}^{b}y(x)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}(x^{2} - 2x + 2)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{0}^{2}x^{2}dx - 2∫_{0}^{2}xdx + 2∫_{0}^{2}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{3}}{3}\\begin{vmatrix}_{0}^{2} - 2 \\cdot \\frac{x^{2}}{2} & _{0}^{2} + 2x\\end{vmatrix}_{0}^{2} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{8}{3} - 0) - 2(2 - 0) + 2(2 - 0) \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{3} - 4 + 4 \\\\\n&= \\frac{8}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186096503 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "6ad216da-1bcf-5b87-9436-918ef1e5015a" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Area Bounded Above a Curve and Below the X-Axis"}] :question/id #uuid "5893a692-573a-5e1a-81aa-db34fa6059ed" :question/math-academy-id "e-782" :question/problem "Find the area of the closed region between the curve $y=x^2-1$ and the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the intersections of the curve with the $x$-axis:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nx^{2} - 1 &= 0 \\\\\nx^{2} &= 1 \\\\\nx &= ± 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe draw a sketch of the region, as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/1040/Source/Images/92bd9521485f93f43b1f96172bd73ba5.png)\n\nWe want the area between the curve $y=x^2-1$ and the $x$-axis, from $x=-1$ to $x=1$. To find the area, we just need to compute the corresponding definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA &= ∫_{a}^{b}y(x)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{1}(x^{2} - 1)dx \\\\\n&= ∫_{-1}^{1}x^{2}dx - ∫_{-1}^{1}dx \\\\\n&= \\frac{x^{3}}{3} \\mid _{-1}^{1} - x \\mid _{-1}^{1} \\\\\n&= (\\frac{1}{3} - (-\\frac{1}{3})) - (1 - (-1)) \\\\\n&= \\frac{2}{3} - 2 \\\\\n&=-\\frac{4}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the region lies below the $x$-axis, we obtained a negative number. So the area that we want is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{3}\n$$"}]}
